**In series** (end to end):
- the **same current** flows through every resistor;
- the potential differences add up: V = V₁ + V₂ + V₃;
- the equivalent resistance is the **sum**: **R = R₁ + R₂ + R₃**, more than the largest one.

**In parallel** (all across the same two points):
- the **same potential difference** is across every resistor;
- the currents add up: I = I₁ + I₂ + I₃;
- **1/R = 1/R₁ + 1/R₂ + 1/R₃**, so the equivalent resistance is **less than the smallest** one.

**Why houses use parallel wiring:** in series, if one appliance fails the whole circuit breaks, and all appliances share the voltage and cannot get the current they each need. In parallel, each appliance gets the full voltage, draws its own current, and can be switched on or off independently. The total resistance also falls.
